!! What it does

Ports turns the `ref` fields of [[kind|$:/plugins/rimir/kind]] instances into connectors you can drag, like the ports of blocks in a graphical editor. You keep working in the normal story river: each instance gets one small handle on its right border, and links are drawn as wires from a port to the target tiddler.

!! How you use it

# ''Hover'' a tiddler — its handle appears on the right border (filled = it has links, the number = how many).
# ''Hover the handle'' — the port flyout slides out: one row per ref field with its icon, caption and current target (or count).
# ''Drag'' a port — a wire follows the pointer; tiddlers that fit the port are outlined; the dock on the right opens with the candidates. Type to filter, Esc cancels.
# ''Release'' on a tiddler or a dock tile — the link is written. Single fields are replaced, multi fields get the target added.
# ''Hover'' a filled port to see wires to its visible targets; ''click'' it to list the targets, each with × to unlink.

!! What it builds on

* Which fields are ports, their captions and icons, what they accept (`ref-types`) and whether they hold one or many targets (`cardinality`) all come from the kind schema.
* Writing both ends of a relation is kind's job: give the field an `inverse` and kind mirrors the link onto the target, whatever wrote it.
* Ports for sq/streams rows come from the separate `rimir/streams-ports` bridge, so ports itself knows nothing about streams.

